## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's understanding of end-to-end testing for multi-step user journeys, including authentication flows, external system mocking, and deterministic test data management. It evaluates practical knowledge of creating reliable, fast, and isolated tests in a real-world SaaS context.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing with authentication and email verification
- Test data seeding and database isolation
- Mocking external services (email delivery, OIDC sign-in)
- Assertions for UI state and created resources
- Time-bounded test design for CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Using Playwright
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('complete onboarding flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed deterministic test data
  await seedTestUser({ email: 'demo@test.com', password: 'Test123!' });

  // Mock email service to capture verification link
  await mockEmailService();

  // Mock OIDC provider
  await mockOIDCProvider();

  // Navigate to signup and complete registration
  await page.goto('/signup');
  await page.fill('[name=email]', 'demo@test.com');
  await page.fill('[name=password]', 'Test123!');
  await page.click('[data-testid=signup-submit]');

  // Extract and follow email verification link
  const verifyLink = await getVerificationLink('demo@test.com');
  await page.goto(verifyLink);

  // Complete profile setup
  await page.fill('[name=name]', 'Jane Doe');
  await page.fill('[name=company]', 'Acme Corp');
  await page.click('[data-testid=profile-submit]');

  // Create first workspace
  await page.fill('[name=workspaceName]', 'My Workspace');
  await page.click('[data-testid=create-workspace]');

  // Assert dashboard displays new workspace
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=workspace-card]')).toContainText('My Workspace');
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for offline-first flows are common in modern web apps and require practical use of network emulation, event queues, and deterministic conflict handling. This question probes hands-on ability to design reliable E2E tests that stay robust under connectivity changes.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Network emulation in Playwright or Cypress
- Backend API mocking and idempotent sync
- Deterministic conflict resolution policy
- Data seeding for repeatable tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test skeleton
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('offline create and sync with deterministic merge', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();
  // seed data via API (omitted for brevity)
  // go offline
  await context.setOffline(true);
  await page.goto('https://app.example');
  await page.fill('#title', 'Offline note');
  await page.fill('#body', 'content');
  await page.click('#save');
  await expect(page.locator('#status')).toHaveText('Queued');
  // go online and sync
  await context.setOffline(false);
  const resp = await page.waitForResponse('/api/notes');
  // assume server returns created note with serverId
  await expect(resp.status()).toBe(201);
  // verify note appears and is synced
  await expect(page.locator('.note-title').last()).toHaveText('Offline note');
  // optional conflict scenario in a second tab would be tested similarly
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test for multiple concurrent offline edits across tabs?
- How would you ensure test reliability when the backend response latency varies?

